#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197981 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d912b296-6fd0-57c2-84e8-ca0b56549756" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Scalar Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ea202ce3-07d4-5324-9ee4-f5617b430772" :question/math-academy-id "e-12477" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\ncalculate $\\boldsymbol f'$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x}$, then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x}$, denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x})$, is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}y) = 2xy, \\mid \\frac{∂f}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}y) = x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2xy & x^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand $\\boldsymbol{f}'$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(-1) & 1^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197982 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a818e04-786a-56b6-90cf-dcb573ccfa64" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ea76d21f-eff8-5634-975f-2b5bd24caf54" :question/math-academy-id "e-12478" :question/problem "For the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^3$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}xy \\\\ y^{2} \\\\ y\\ln x\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nthe total derivative $\\boldsymbol f'$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A A & x \\\\ A A & 2y \\\\ \\frac{y}{x} & A A\\end{bmatrix}$\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x}$, then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x}$, denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x})$, is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{3}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f_{3}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) = y, & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy) = x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y^{2}) = 0, & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2}) = 2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{3}}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\ln x) = \\frac{y}{x}, & \\frac{∂f_{3}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\ln x) = \\ln x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y & x \\\\ 0 & 2y \\\\ \\frac{y}{x} & ln ⁡ x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThus, the missing entries are $y,\\:$ $0,\\:$ and $\\ln{x}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197983 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e90349c-33ae-5b60-9d0d-7542dfebbbc7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Plane to the Graph of a Scalar Function in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "db0be438-94fe-52de-9daf-60d1727ce7b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12479" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nfind the missing values." :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x}$, then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x}$, denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x})$, is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{3} + e^{y}) = 3x^{2}, \\mid \\frac{∂f}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{3} + e^{y}) = e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} & e^{y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & e^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nz - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(\\mathbf a)$, $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)$, and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & e 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197984 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f09fc28-bdce-554f-98f5-019d34542a13" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Affine Approximation of a Vector Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f3540571-9159-50b4-a46c-4cf28a454602" :question/math-academy-id "e-12484" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2}\\sin x_{1} \\\\ \\sin x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}π \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nwhat are the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x}$, then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x}$, denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x})$, is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x_{1}}(x_{2}\\sin x_{1}) = x_{2}\\cos x_{1}, & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x_{2}}(x_{2}\\sin x_{1}) = \\sin x_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x_{1}}(\\sin x_{2}) = 0, & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x_{2}}(\\sin x_{2}) = \\cos x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2}\\cos x_{1} & \\sin x_{1} \\\\ 0 & \\cos x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvaluating $\\boldsymbol{f}$ and its derivative at $\\begin{bmatrix}π \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}\\left(\\mathbf x \\right) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a)$, $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)$, and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a818e04-786a-56b6-90cf-dcb573ccfa64" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84a02a54-5df2-54f3-bf70-6fa9b47be689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y \\\\ ye^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194256} {:answer/id #uuid "aba6b2d2-aff8-59c9-9848-36111e68c66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}ye^{xy} & 2x \\\\ 2y & xe^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194257} {:answer/id #uuid "efa88cd6-7e18-5c6a-8ca8-e8ec1ddd543c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2x \\\\ xe^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194258} {:answer/id #uuid "50267c0b-dd49-55b6-88d9-96cb362c6ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x \\\\ ye^{xy} & xe^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}